<main>

    <section class="hero">
        <h1 class="fade-up fade-up-2">Deploy to DigitalOcean</h1>
        <p class="tagline fade-up fade-up-3">Open the Console, paste one line, watch it run. It prints your token + URL. Never SSH. Own every byte.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>The web-console path</h2>

        <p>The simplest way to stand up a real, always-on Parachute: create a droplet, open its <strong>Console</strong> in your browser, paste <strong>one line</strong>, and watch it run. When it finishes it prints your <strong>bootstrap token</strong> and <strong>URL</strong> right there in the Console &mdash; open the URL, set your username + password <strong>in your browser</strong>, done. <strong>No SSH, no separate token hunt.</strong> The droplet self-installs hub + vault and gets a real Let's Encrypt certificate via <a href="https://caddyserver.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Caddy</a> &mdash; all on first run.</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Always on.</strong> A $6/mo Linux box <em>you</em> rent doesn't sleep when your laptop does &mdash; your vault stays reachable around the clock.</li>
            <li><strong>Your password never touches the cloud.</strong> No admin username or password is ever typed into the cloud config. You set it in the browser at <code>/admin/setup</code> using a one-time token the install prints &mdash; so nothing sensitive persists in DigitalOcean's metadata, User Data, or droplet console history.</li>
            <li><strong>Fully yours.</strong> Hub + vault run on your box; Caddy on the same box terminates TLS. No tenancy, no shared database, no Parachute-operated cloud between you and your data. Everything lives on your disk under <code>~/.parachute/</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Real public HTTPS, no DNS chore.</strong> Bring a domain if you have one; otherwise the install derives a working HTTPS hostname from the droplet's IP automatically (via <code>sslip.io</code>) so Let's Encrypt just works.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Just want to kick the tires? <a href="/start/">Install it on your laptop</a> first &mdash; same stack, free, but it sleeps when your machine does. Come back here when you want an always-on home.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="zero-ssh">
        <h2>1. Create an Ubuntu 24.04 droplet</h2>

        <p>In the DigitalOcean console (<a href="https://cloud.digitalocean.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cloud.digitalocean.com</a>) &rarr; <strong>Create &rarr; Droplets</strong>:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Image:</strong> Ubuntu 24.04 (LTS) x64.</li>
            <li><strong>Size:</strong> $6/mo (1 GB) for hub + vault. Pick <strong>$12/mo (2 GB)</strong> if you want on-box transcription (Scribe) or to run Claude Code on the box.</li>
            <li><strong>Region:</strong> nearest you.</li>
            <li><strong>Authentication:</strong> an SSH key is <em>optional</em> &mdash; for this path you won't need it.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Click <strong>Create</strong> and give it ~30 seconds to come up.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="finish-setup">
        <h2>2. Open the Console, paste one line, finish in your browser</h2>

        <ol>
            <li><strong>Open the droplet's Console.</strong> In the DigitalOcean dashboard, on your droplet's page click <strong>Access &rarr; Launch Droplet Console</strong> &mdash; a root terminal right in your browser, no SSH needed. (If it prompts for a password, set one on that Access tab first.)</li>
            <li><strong>Paste this one line and run it:</strong>
                <div class="code-inline" style="text-align:left; margin-top:0.6rem;">
<code>curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/digitalocean.sh | bash</code>
                </div>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Wait ~2&ndash;3 minutes.</strong> It installs Bun, hub + vault, and Caddy, then fetches a TLS cert on the first HTTPS hit. When it finishes it prints your <strong>bootstrap token</strong> (<code>parachute-bootstrap-&hellip;</code>) and your <strong>setup URL</strong> right there in the Console.</li>
            <li><strong>Open that URL in your browser</strong> &mdash; it lands on <code>/admin/setup</code>. With a domain it's <code>https://&lt;your-domain&gt;/admin/setup</code>; without one, <code>https://&lt;droplet-ip&gt;.sslip.io/admin/setup</code> (your droplet's public IPv4, dots and all, e.g. <code>https://203.0.113.5.sslip.io/admin/setup</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Paste the bootstrap token, then choose your own username + password.</strong> That's it &mdash; you're the owner, and your password was set in the browser, never stored in the cloud config. <a href="#connect">Connect your AI</a> next.</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="muted" style="font-size:0.9em;">The token is <strong>single-use</strong> and regenerates if the hub restarts. To re-read the current one from the Console, run <code>cat parachute-setup.txt</code>, or:</p>
        <div class="code-inline">
<code>curl -fsS -H 'Accept: application/json' http://127.0.0.1:1939/admin/setup | grep -oE 'parachute-bootstrap-[A-Za-z0-9_-]+' | head -1</code>
        </div>

        <p class="muted" style="font-size:0.9em;">First load can take a few extra seconds while Let's Encrypt issues the cert. If the page doesn't come up after ~5 minutes, see <a href="#pitfalls">Pitfalls</a> (or use the <a href="#manual">SSH path</a> to watch the install log line by line).</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="domain">
        <h2>No domain needed &mdash; or bring your own</h2>

        <p><strong>No domain needed.</strong> Run it as-is and you get a working HTTPS address at <code>https://&lt;your-droplet-ip&gt;.sslip.io</code> automatically. <a href="https://sslip.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sslip.io</a> is free wildcard DNS that resolves that name straight to your droplet, so the URL <em>and</em> the Let's Encrypt cert just work with <strong>zero DNS setup</strong>. Perfect for getting started, and you can switch to a custom domain anytime.</p>

        <p><strong>Using your own domain?</strong> A domain you own is sturdier for anything long-lived: every <code>sslip.io</code> host shares <em>one</em> Let's Encrypt registered-domain rate limit, so a busy day across all users can hit the cap. To use yours:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Point it at the droplet first.</strong> In your DNS provider add an <strong>A record</strong> for the hostname you want (e.g. <code>vault.example.com</code>) &rarr; your droplet's public IPv4 (shown in the DO dashboard).</li>
            <li><strong>Run it with the domain set:</strong>
                <div class="code-inline" style="text-align:left; margin-top:0.6rem;">
<code>PARACHUTE_DOMAIN=vault.example.com curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/digitalocean.sh | bash</code>
                </div>
                (or <code>export PARACHUTE_DOMAIN=vault.example.com</code> first). Caddy fetches a Let's Encrypt cert for it automatically.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>(If DNS hasn't propagated when the script runs, just re-run the <code>curl &hellip; | bash</code> once it has &mdash; it's idempotent.)</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="unattended">
        <h2>Prefer unattended / Infrastructure-as-Code?</h2>

        <p>Paste the <em>same</em> one-liner into the droplet's <strong>User Data</strong> when you create it (Advanced options &rarr; <strong>Add Initialization scripts</strong>), prefixed with a <code>#!/bin/bash</code> line &mdash; it self-installs on first boot, no Console step:</p>
        <div class="code-inline" style="text-align:left;">
<code>#!/bin/bash<br>
curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/digitalocean.sh | bash</code>
        </div>
        <p>To pin a domain in this path, add it on its own <code>export</code> line above the <code>curl</code>:</p>
        <div class="code-inline" style="text-align:left;">
<code>#!/bin/bash<br>
export PARACHUTE_DOMAIN=vault.example.com<br>
curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/digitalocean.sh | bash</code>
        </div>
        <p>Then grab your bootstrap token once the box is up &mdash; over SSH:</p>
        <div class="code-inline">
<code>ssh root@&lt;your-droplet-ip&gt; cat parachute-setup.txt</code>
        </div>
        <p>(or open the Console and <code>cat parachute-setup.txt</code>), copy the token + setup URL, and finish in your browser as above.</p>

        <aside class="upgrade-callout">
            <strong>Why a <code>#!/bin/bash</code> block (not <code>#cloud-config</code>)?</strong>
            <p>A plain shell script runs as <em>one</em> shell, so the optional <code>export PARACHUTE_DOMAIN</code> line is in scope when the install script runs. (A <code>#cloud-config</code> <code>runcmd:</code> list runs each line as a <em>separate</em> shell, so exports there wouldn't carry over to the <code>curl | bash</code>.) Keep the <code>#!/bin/bash</code> first line exactly as shown. No username or password ever goes in here &mdash; you set it in the browser.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="doctl">
        <h2>Drive it from the CLI (doctl)</h2>
        <p>If you script DigitalOcean (<a href="https://docs.digitalocean.com/reference/doctl/how-to/install/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">doctl</a>), put the same User Data in a file and create the droplet in one command. Save the <code>#!/bin/bash</code> block from the <a href="#unattended">unattended path</a> (no credentials in it) as <code>parachute-user-data.sh</code>, then:</p>
        <div class="code-inline" style="text-align:left;">
<code>doctl compute droplet create parachute \
  --image ubuntu-24-04-x64 --size s-1vcpu-1gb --region nyc3 \
  --user-data-file parachute-user-data.sh --wait</code>
        </div>
        <p><code>--wait</code> blocks until the droplet is Active; give it the usual couple of minutes after that, then open your URL. (Add <code>--ssh-keys &lt;fingerprint&gt;</code> if you want SSH access too &mdash; not required.)</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="connect">
        <h2>3. Connect your AI</h2>

        <p>Point any MCP client at your vault:</p>
        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>https://&lt;your-host&gt;/vault/default/mcp</code>
        </div>
        <p>(<code>default</code> is your vault name unless you changed it; the <code>/mcp</code> suffix is required.) The admin SPA's <strong>Connect</strong> toggle on each vault row hands you a copy-paste <code>claude mcp add</code> command with a fresh token, plus per-client steps for Claude.ai, ChatGPT, Codex, Goose, and others. Then try the two starter prompts: the <a href="/onboarding/vault-setup/">set-up-your-vault prompt</a> (connect your AI and say &ldquo;help me set up my parachute&rdquo;) to fill your vault, then the <a href="/onboarding/surface-build/">build-a-surface prompt</a> to shape a custom view over it.</p>

        <aside class="upgrade-callout">
            <strong>Claude connector fails right after consent?</strong>
            <p>If you put Cloudflare (or another edge/WAF) <em>in front</em> of your domain, its bot protection can <code>403</code> Claude's server-to-server token exchange &mdash; consent passes in your browser, then the integration "rejected the credentials." This isn't a Parachute bug. With the Caddy-direct path here there's no Cloudflare edge in the way, so you typically won't hit it. If you do front the box with Cloudflare, turn off Bot Fight Mode / Browser Integrity Check for the zone (Security &rarr; Bots), or add a WAF Skip rule for <code>/oauth/*</code>, <code>/.well-known/*</code>, and <code>/vault/*</code>. Confirm with <code>curl -A python-requests https://&lt;host&gt;/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server</code>.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>

    <hr style="margin: 3rem 0; border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--border);">

    <section class="section" id="manual">
        <h2>Prefer to watch it install? The SSH path</h2>

        <p>Want to read the script before piping it to your shell, or watch it install line by line? Same install, run by hand &mdash; this is also the fallback if the zero-SSH boot didn't come up.</p>

        <h3>1. Create a droplet (Ubuntu 24.04, add your SSH key)</h3>
        <p>Same sizing as above. Add your SSH public key at creation, note the public IPv4, wait ~30 seconds.</p>

        <h3>2. SSH in and run it</h3>
        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>ssh root@&lt;your-droplet-ip&gt;</code>
        </div>
        <p>Then run the install:</p>
        <div class="code-inline">
<code>curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/digitalocean.sh | bash</code>
        </div>
        <p>To pin your own domain, point an A record at the droplet (see <a href="#domain">above</a>) and prefix the command with <code>PARACHUTE_DOMAIN=vault.example.com</code> (or <code>export</code> it first).</p>
        <p>The script, on a fresh droplet:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Installs <strong>Bun</strong> + <code>@openparachute/hub</code> + <code>@openparachute/vault</code>.</li>
            <li>Derives your public hostname (your domain, or <code>&lt;ip&gt;.sslip.io</code>).</li>
            <li>Runs <code>parachute init --hub-origin https://&lt;host&gt;</code> &mdash; persists the public origin <em>before</em> modules start, installs + starts the hub as a <code>systemd</code> unit (survives reboots), installs the vault.</li>
            <li>Installs + configures <strong>Caddy</strong> to terminate Let's Encrypt TLS and reverse-proxy to the loopback hub.</li>
            <li>Reads the hub's one-time <strong>bootstrap token</strong> and writes it (plus your <code>/admin/setup</code> URL) into <code>/root/parachute-setup.txt</code> &mdash; so you set your own password in the browser, not on the command line.</li>
            <li>Decides transcription by RAM (see <a href="#transcription">below</a>).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>It's <strong>idempotent</strong> &mdash; safe to re-run; each step skips itself if already done. It logs each step clearly, so a live run is easy to follow. When it finishes, it prints your bootstrap token + setup URL right in the terminal.</p>

        <h3 id="no-creds">Finish setup &mdash; set your password in the browser</h3>
        <p>The install never asks for a password &mdash; it leaves a summary at <code>/root/parachute-setup.txt</code> with the public URL, the one-time bootstrap token, and how to finish. Read it:</p>
        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>cat /root/parachute-setup.txt</code>
        </div>
        <p>Copy the <code>parachute-bootstrap-&hellip;</code> token and the setup URL, then open <code>https://&lt;your-host&gt;/admin/setup</code> in your browser, paste the token, and choose your username + password. The token is single-use and regenerates on hub restart; re-read the current one with:</p>
        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>curl -fsS -H 'Accept: application/json' http://127.0.0.1:1939/admin/setup | grep -oE 'parachute-bootstrap-[A-Za-z0-9_-]+' | head -1</code>
        </div>
        <p class="muted" style="font-size:0.9em;">If <code>parachute</code> isn't found in a fresh SSH session, open a new shell (or run <code>export PATH="$HOME/.bun/bin:$PATH"</code>) so the freshly-installed binary is on your PATH.</p>

        <aside class="upgrade-callout">
            <strong>A true 1-click DigitalOcean Marketplace image is on the roadmap.</strong>
            <p>A pre-baked Marketplace droplet &mdash; pick "Parachute" from the image list, no User Data to paste &mdash; is planned. Until then, the cloud-init path above is the closest thing: paste one block and the droplet self-installs and certs on first boot, then you set your password in the browser.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="transcription">
        <h2>Transcription &mdash; decided by RAM</h2>
        <p>The install picks a sensible default for voice transcription (Scribe) based on the box:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>≥2 GB RAM</strong> (the $12 droplet) &mdash; it installs the <strong>local</strong> Scribe engine on-box (runs entirely on your hardware, no API key). Set <code>PARACHUTE_TRANSCRIBE=none</code> to skip it.</li>
            <li><strong>&lt;2 GB RAM</strong> (the $6 droplet) &mdash; local ASR is too tight, so it's skipped. Add a <strong>cloud</strong> provider key (e.g. <a href="https://groq.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Groq</a>, ~$0.04/hr) later in the admin UI, or resize to 2 GB. Force this with <code>PARACHUTE_TRANSCRIBE=cloud</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Transcription setup is <strong>non-fatal</strong> &mdash; if the engine install hiccups, the rest of your hub still comes up fine; you just add transcription from the admin UI afterward.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="alt-ingress">
        <h2>Alternative ingress (advanced)</h2>

        <p>The Caddy-direct path above is the recommended ingress &mdash; clean public HTTPS, no third party in the request path. If you specifically prefer a tunnel (e.g. you don't want to open ports 80/443, or you want a private tailnet-only hub), these still work. They're not the default.</p>

        <h3>Cloudflare Tunnel</h3>
        <p>A tunnel instead of Caddy &mdash; no inbound ports open; needs a domain on a Cloudflare zone (free tier fine). SSH in and run:</p>
        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>parachute expose public --cloudflare --domain vault.example.com</code>
        </div>
        <p>The apex (<code>example.com</code>) must already be a Cloudflare zone. <strong>Expose <em>creates</em> the tunnel</strong> &mdash; don't pre-create it. Each machine gets its own per-hostname tunnel name (no account-wide collision); the <code>cloudflared</code> connector installs as its own managed unit and returns after a reboot. Note: Cloudflare's Bot Fight Mode can break the Claude connector (see the box in <a href="#connect">Connect your AI</a>).</p>

        <h3>Tailscale Funnel &mdash; no domain, private-leaning</h3>
        <p>If you'd rather not own a domain or open ports, set up <a href="https://tailscale.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tailscale</a> on the droplet, then:</p>
        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>parachute expose public</code>
        </div>
        <p>That gives you a <code>*.ts.net</code> URL on your tailnet.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="pitfalls">
        <h2>Pitfalls</h2>

        <p>Most of these you'll never hit.</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>URL not up after a few minutes?</strong> On first boot, cloud-init runs after the network and apt are ready, so the install can take a couple of minutes; Caddy then needs a moment to fetch the TLS cert on the first HTTPS hit. If it's still down after ~5 minutes, SSH in and check: <code>cat /root/parachute-setup.txt</code> (the install summary), <code>tail -f /var/log/cloud-init-output.log</code> (the full install log), and <code>systemctl status caddy</code> / <code>journalctl -u caddy</code> (the TLS terminator).</li>
            <li><strong>Ports 80 + 443 must be reachable.</strong> Caddy needs them for the Let's Encrypt challenge and to serve HTTPS. DO droplets have no firewall by default &mdash; fine. If you enabled <code>ufw</code>, run <code>ufw allow 80,443/tcp</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>No bootstrap token in the summary?</strong> If <code>parachute-setup.txt</code> says "already set up" instead of showing a token, this box already has an owner &mdash; just sign in at <code>/login</code>. The token only appears while no admin exists. To create the first owner, re-read the current token (it regenerates on hub restart) with the loopback <code>curl &hellip; /admin/setup</code> one-liner above and paste it at <code>/admin/setup</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>sslip.io rate limit.</strong> If Caddy can't get a cert for an <code>&lt;ip&gt;.sslip.io</code> host, the shared Let's Encrypt rate limit may be temporarily hit &mdash; <a href="#domain">bring your own domain</a> (its own budget) and re-run.</li>
            <li><strong>Bun 1.3+ required.</strong> The setup script installs the latest. Run <code>parachute</code> commands in a <em>fresh</em> shell so <code>~/.bun/bin</code> is on your PATH (the script appends it to <code>~/.bashrc</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Non-root reboot survival needs linger.</strong> The script installs as root by default (a system unit, reboot-safe). If you installed as a non-root user, run <code>sudo loginctl enable-linger $USER</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep current.</strong> <code>parachute upgrade</code> (add <code>--channel rc</code> for release candidates). DB migrations run on the first post-upgrade boot.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Cost</h2>

        <p>All figures <em>approximate</em> &mdash; check current DigitalOcean pricing.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Droplet:</strong> $6/mo (1 GB) for hub + vault; $12/mo (2 GB) for on-box transcription or to run Claude Code on the box.</li>
            <li><strong>TLS:</strong> free &mdash; Caddy provisions Let's Encrypt certs automatically. <code>sslip.io</code> hostnames are free and need no DNS setup; a domain you own is sturdier for production (and just the cost of the domain).</li>
        </ul>
        <p><em>Compare:</em> the same stack on your own Mac or Linux box is free, but you give up the always-on URL and the "works from my phone, anywhere" property. The $6 droplet is that trade, owned end to end.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>What you get</h2>

        <p>One <code>systemd</code> unit running the hub on :1939 &mdash; with reboot survival and admin-UI module management &mdash; supervising your vault as a child process, with <strong>Caddy</strong> in front terminating public HTTPS. The script installs <strong>hub + vault</strong> (and optionally local transcription); add <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-surface" target="_blank">Surface</a> (the UI host &mdash; <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-surface/tree/main/packages/notes-ui" target="_blank">Notes</a> auto-bootstraps on its first install) and <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-scribe" target="_blank">Scribe</a> later from the admin UI, and the hub supervises them as child processes too. Everything lives under <code>~/.parachute/</code> on the droplet's own disk &mdash; you fully own it. See the <a href="/design/2026-05-18-v06-deploy-architecture/">v0.6 deploy architecture note</a> for the full shape.</p>
    </section>

